              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.327,
                0.662,
                0.672,
                0.677
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Figure 1: From left to right, models 1, 2, and 3"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.231,
            0.696,
            0.785,
            0.774
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "b) Why were the other two networks unsatisfactory models? Explain the deficiencies of each in terms of the conditional independence and dependence relationships they model. Which one of the remaining models represents an equivalent joint probability table as the best model, given that the description of the relationships was accurate?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.233,
            0.782,
            0.786,
            0.845
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "c) I found that tiredness and having the flu each have a small impact on the likelihood of studying (small because MIT students are so tough). Draw a network that expresses this connection. Compute its complexity and the complexity of the network you choose in part a). Give two reasons why"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.494,
            0.877,
            0.504,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.159,
            0.783,
            0.19
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "the original network is superior, despite the small improvement this new network gives in predictive power."
            }
          ],
          "continues_prev": true
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.199,
            0.784,
            0.245
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "d) Leslie got the flu. Using model 3, compute the probability that she will fail the exam, in terms of values that are available in the conditional probability tables stored at each node."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.233,
            0.255,
            0.785,
            0.3
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "e) Michael passed the exam. Using model 3, compute the probability that he studied, in terms of values that are available in the conditional probability tables stored at each node."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.322,
            0.41,
            0.34
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2 True & False"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.353,
            0.666,
            0.369
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. For any A and B "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "P ( A ) = P ( A | B ) P ( B ) + P ( \\neg A | B ) P ( B )"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.234,
            0.379,
            0.782,
            0.409
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Every continuous function can be computed by some multi-layer perceptron with arbitrary unit functions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.234,
            0.419,
            0.726,
            0.435
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Bayes’ nets only represent causal relationships between variables."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.444,
            0.505,
            0.46
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. For any A and B "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "P(A)>P(A,B)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.234,
            0.47,
            0.782,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Perceptrons are sensitive to their learning rate when learning linearly separable classes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.234,
            0.51,
            0.782,
            0.54
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. If we use mean-squared error to train a perceptron, we will always successfully separate linearly separable classes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.234,
            0.55,
            0.594,
            0.565
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. Over-fitting is only a problem with noisy data."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.234,
            0.575,
            0.624,
            0.59
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8. If A and B are independent "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "P(A,B)=P(A)P(B)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.234,
            0.6,
            0.782,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9. Multi-layer perceptrons are equivalent to single-layer perceptrons unless they contain non-linear hidden units."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.653,
            0.543,
            0.672
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3 The Prisoner’s Dilemma"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.685,
            0.784,
            0.746
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The Prisoner’s Dilemma is a well-known problem in game theory. Two thieves (partners in crime) are arrested and held in custody separately. The police offer each the same deal. Inform on your partner and we will reduce your sentence. The following outcomes and costs are possible:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.757,
            0.784,
            0.788
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. If both you and your partner stay quiet, you will both be convicted of misdemeanor offenses (lesser charges). The cost of this is 10."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.234,
            0.798,
            0.783,
            0.828
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. If you turn state’s evidence (cooperate with the police), you will be convicted of a misdemeanor and fined. The cost of this is 50."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.494,
            0.877,
            0.505,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.234,
            0.159,
            0.784,
            0.19
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. If you do not cooperate, but your partner does, you will be convicted of a felony (a major crime). The cost of this is 100."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.201,
            0.785,
            0.277
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The dilemma is that the best course of action is for both of you to stay quiet, but since there is no honor among thieves, you do not trust one another. Then you both will turn state’s evidence in order to avoid being convicted of the major crime (which happens if your partner turns state’s evidence and you do not)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.277,
            0.785,
            0.37
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Consider this twist. Before you are hauled away, you and your partner swear to keep quiet. You believe that there is a 60% chance that he will keep his word. Draw a decision tree that represents your decision (to keep quiet or to cooperate) and the possible outcomes. What decision has the highest expected value? If x represents the probability that your partner will keep quiet, for what value of x is the value of keeping quiet equal to the value of cooperating?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.39,
            0.372,
            0.41
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4 Searching"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.422,
            0.784,
            0.483
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "a) I am an explorer looking for a new route to India. Assuming the Earth is flat (I’m not a very bright explorer), and I know the straight-line distances between any two cities on Earth, what would be a good search strategy for finding the shortest route?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.231,
            0.492,
            0.784,
            0.539
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "b) Just before I am about to leave, I run into Magellan and he informs me that the Earth is spherical and that my straight-line distances are underestimates. Should I keep the same search strategy? Why or why not?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.56,
            0.388,
            0.58
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5 Overfitting"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.591,
            0.784,
            0.668
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We have discussed many types of machine learning including regression (fitting a function to data points), generative models (Bayes’ nets, for example), and classification (perceptrons, multi-layer perceptrons, support vector machines). Pick two types of machine learning and describe how overfitting can occur and how we can modify their error functions to avoid it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.69,
            0.364,
            0.71
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6 Planning"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.72,
            0.784,
            0.752
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If GraphPlan terminates with a successful, 3-action plan in the first iteration what constraints are there on the order in which the actions must be executed?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.773,
            0.701,
            0.793
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7 GraphPlan Vs. Partial Order Planner"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.805,
            0.444,
            0.82
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Consider the following domain:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.494,
            0.877,
            0.505,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
